156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 680 0 R
/E (In this Arduino program, we first define the pins using #define. The LDR sensor is connected to pin 2 \(LDR_PIN\) and the LED is connected to pin 3 \(LED_PIN\). This makes the code easier to read and use.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 681 0 R
/E (Next, the setup\(\) function runs only once when the Arduino starts. Inside this, we use pinMode\(\) to set the LDR pin as INPUT and the LED pin as OUTPUT. We also use digitalWrite\(\) to make sure the LED is initially turned OFF.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 682 0 R
/E (After that, the loop\(\) function runs continuously. Inside the loop, we read the LDR sensor value using digitalRead\(\) and store it in a variable called ldrstate.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 683 0 R
/E (Then, we use an if-else condition to control the LED. If the LDR detects light \(HIGH\), the LED turns ON. Otherwise, the LED turns OFF.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (So overall, the Arduino continuously checks the light level using the LDR and controls the LED based on the light intensity.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
