/T (Code Explanation)
/E (Code Explanation)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
248 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 709 0 R
/E (In this Arduino program, we first define the pins using #define. The LDR sensor is connected to pin 2 \(LDR_PIN\), and two LEDs are connected to pins 6 \(LED_PIN1\) and 7 \(LED_PIN2\). The second LED works in the opposite way to the first LED.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 710 0 R
/E (Next, the setup\(\) function runs only once when the Arduino starts. Inside setup, we use pinMode\(\) to set the LDR pin as INPUT and both LED pins as OUTPUT. We also use digitalWrite\(\) to turn both LEDs OFF initially.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
250 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 711 0 R
/E (After that, the loop\(\) function runs continuously. Inside the loop, we read the LDR sensor value using digitalRead\(\) and store it in a variable called ldrstate.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [26]
>>
endobj
251 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 712 0 R
/E (Then, we use an if-else condition to control both LEDs. If the LDR detects light \(HIGH\), the LED on pin 6 turns ON and the LED on pin 7 turns OFF. If there is no light \(LOW\), the LED on pin 6 turns OFF and the LED on pin 7 turns ON.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
/E (So overall, the Arduino keeps checking the light level and controls the two LEDs in opposite ways based on the light intensity.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
